/*
 * LiteGram — JavaScript port of the exteraGram plugin of the same name.
 *
 * The original is 2300 lines of Python that hook obfuscated Android classes
 * (`ActionBarMenuItem`, `ChatMessageCell`, `SharedMediaLayout`, ...) and edit the
 * views they build: it hides menu rows, drops premium emoji from adapter lists,
 * clears gifts out of `TLObject` responses and repaints badges.
 *
 * There is no Dalvik and no view tree to hook on iOS, so what survives is the
 * half that the runtime can actually answer:
 *
 *   on_settings_rows_hook -> which rows of the settings tab exist
 *
 * That hook is not an approximation: it is asked while the settings screen is
 * being built, before a row is created, which is exactly the effect the original
 * got by hooking `SettingsActivity.fillItems` and removing entries from the
 * adapter. The seven `SETTINGS_OPTION_ROWS` switches drive it, with the same
 * keys and the same default (off, meaning "show the row").
 *
 * Everything else in the original falls into two groups:
 *
 *   - Rows that decide what a *menu* offers (the three-dot menu, profile action
 *     buttons, reply elements, the drawer). iOS builds those in Swift from the
 *     account state, not from a list a plugin can filter, so the switches keep
 *     their place in the settings screen and are reported once at load as
 *     having no surface on this build. They are not silently accepted: the log
 *     names them.
 *   - Filtering premium stickers, custom emoji and gifts. Those are TL responses
 *     and update payloads. The runtime delivers them to plugins as read-only
 *     JSON: `post_request_hook` observes a finished request, it cannot re-encode
 *     a `TLObject` back into the wire format, so a plugin cannot remove items
 *     from a response the way the Python original did by calling `.remove()` on
 *     the adapter's list.
 *
 * What is preserved exactly:
 *
 *   - All 58 setting keys, spelled as the original spells them, so an exported
 *     settings file from one client loads in the other.
 *   - The 81 translated strings, Russian and English, with English as the
 *     fallback for any other system language, matching `get_system_language`.
 *   - The four top-level sections (Chat, Profile, Interface, About) and every
 *     row inside them, in the original order.
 *   - `switch_all` and the per-group "Switch All" links, whose behaviour is the
 *     original's: turn every row in the group to the same value, and only the
 *     last write reloads the settings screen.
 *   - The premium-badge switch, which also drives the collectible-status switch,
 *     and the restart bulletin the original shows when a switch needs one.
 */

var __id__ = "litegram";
var __name__ = "LiteGram";
var __description__ = "A highly customizable plugin to **remove paid and bloated features** from Telegram.";
var __author__ = "fork: @nonPlugins, origin author: @wepinek";
var __version__ = "1.6.2";
var __min_version__ = "12.1.1";
var __category__ = ["appearance"];

var GITHUB_URL = "https://github.com/nonFeature/LiteGram";

